    </p>

    <h2>How the history pages are written</h2>
    <p>
      The history pages try to show the evidence fairly, whatever the reader believes. Each claim is tied to a source.
      Anything first reported by writers after the period is labeled as later tradition. Where scholars disagree, the
      page names the views and who holds them, rather than picking one. Where Christian tradition reads a passage in a
      particular way, the page says so and attributes it.
    </p>

    <h2>How pages are checked</h2>
    <p>
      Pages are drafted with the help of an AI and then checked against their sources. Each page shows whether that
      review has happened yet: a page marked "Draft" has not been fully reviewed, and its banner says what has been
      checked so far. The full list of sources and their licenses is on the
      <a href="/history/sources/">Sources and permissions</a> page.
    </p>
    <h3>The AI source check</h3>
    <p>
      Every page is being checked against its sources by Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model made by Anthropic. Each citation,
      quotation, date, name and figure is either checked against its source or rewritten or removed. A claim the sources
      do not support is cut back to what they do say, not left in with a label. The check is done one era at a time, and
      each era's banner says when its check was done. The check is under way: the first eleven eras, from the Apostolic Age
      to the eve of the French Revolution in 1789, were checked in November 2026, and the other eras have not yet been checked. When every page has been checked, this section will give the
      month the whole review was completed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Quotations and references to ancient writers are read against the full text of the public-domain translations
      named on each page. Statements about what modern scholars think are tied to a named book. Those books are in
      copyright, so the AI could not read them; the site's owner checks each statement against the book and records the
      page number. Until that is done, the statement is marked in the review ledger as not yet checked by a person.
    </p>
    <p>Sources the AI check could not read in full so far:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Raymond E. Brown, <i>An Introduction to the New Testament</i>; Henry Chadwick, <i>The Early Church</i>; Candida
        Moss, <i>The Myth of Persecution</i>; Robert Louis Wilken, <i>The Christians as the Romans Saw Them</i>; Harry Y. Gamble, <i>The
        New Testament Canon</i>; the books by Hurtado, Bauckham, Dunn, Ehrman, and Becker and Reed named on the
        Apostolic Age page; Carl H. Kraeling, <i>The Christian Building</i>, the Dura-Europos excavation report named
        on the After the Apostles page; and the further reading on the Age of the Councils and After Chalcedon pages (Williams,
        Peter Brown, Kelly, Barnes, Cameron, Fletcher, Wood). All are in copyright, and the statements that rest on them are on the owner's checklist.
      </li>
      <li>The Gallio inscription at Delphi, read only through published descriptions, not an edition.</li>
      <li>
        The Dura-Europos Christian building, checked only through the Yale University Art Gallery's online record of one
        of its paintings, not the excavation report.
      </li>
      <li>
        Muslim sources for the beginnings of Islam and the Arab conquests. The From Gregory to the Second Council of
        Nicaea page gives those events only as Gibbon's Decline and Fall reports them.
      </li>
      <li>
        Eastern sources for the quarrels between Rome and Constantinople, such as the letters of Photius and Michael
        Cerularius, and the Caroline Books themselves. The From Charlemagne to the Great Schism page gives those
        events from Western sources, the Catholic Encyclopedia of 1913 and Gibbon, and says so.
      </li>
      <li>
        The crusade chronicles (the Gesta Francorum, Raymond of Aguilers, Fulcher of Chartres in full, Ambroise, Odo of
        Deuil and William of Tyre), and Muslim and Byzantine accounts of the crusades.
        The Reform and Crusade page and its maps give the crusades from Villehardouin, the source books, Schaff's
        History of the Christian Church and the Catholic Encyclopedia, and say so.
      </li>
      <li>
        The writings of Thomas Aquinas, John Wyclif and Jan Hus, and the decrees of the Council of Constance. The From the
        Friars to the Council of Constance page describes them from Schaff's History of the Christian Church and the
        Catholic Encyclopedia.
      </li>
      <li>
        The decrees of the councils of Basel and Florence, The Imitation of Christ, and Burchard's diary of the papal
        court. The Late Medieval Church page describes them from Schaff and the Catholic Encyclopedia.
      </li>
      <li>
        The writings of Luther and Calvin, the decrees of the Council of Trent, and the treaties of Augsburg (1555) and
        Westphalia (1648). The Reformation page describes them from Schaff, the Catholic Encyclopedia and Schiller.
      </li>
      <li>
        Spener's Pia Desideria, Kant's writings, the text of Hume's Dialogues beyond its title page, and the French
        decrees of 1789 and 1790. The Reason and Revival page describes them from Schaff and the Catholic Encyclopedia.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each check is recorded, with the passage that settled it, in the era's review ledger, for example the
      <a href="https://github.com/githubacct54385/Cairnway/blob/main/docs/REVIEW-apostolic-age.md">Apostolic Age ledger</a>.
      No specialist has reviewed any page yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every review record is public. The project's <a href="https://github.com/githubacct54385/Cairnway/tree/main/docs">docs folder</a>
      holds the review ledgers, which list each claim, how far it was checked, and what the check found. The plans for each
      era, and the process for checking sources, are there too.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you spot a mistake, use the "Report a problem" link at the end of the era, or
      <a href="/history/report/">the report form</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
